{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>All these are the tribes, etc.</b> One needs to know the count of twelve — does it include Levi? If so, the verse counts Joseph as only one, and Jacob said to him (Genesis 48:5): ״אֶפְרַיִם וּמְנַשֶּׁה כִּרְאוּבֵן וְשִׁמְעוֹן.״ And if Levi is not included, as we find that the verse did not count him in the tally of the twelve tribes under the banners, three per banner, it is difficult: why should Levi be diminished from his father's blessing? And if it is because we find for him only rebuke and he said only the blessed ones — if so, Reuben and Simeon too would not enter the count." "Or perhaps the verse did not begin to count Ephraim and Manasseh as two until after the exodus from Egypt, whereas here the verse counts Joseph — and this is a difficulty. What appears correct to say is that since the verse said ״each man according to his blessing he blessed,״ etc., and we find that Jacob included Simeon and Levi in one category, therefore it counts the two of them as one:" "<b>which he spoke, etc., and he blessed, etc.</b> Even though we saw that he did not bless Reuben, nor Simeon, nor Levi, the verse tells that his harsh words to them are their blessing — which is what it means by <b>which he spoke</b>, etc., <b>and he blessed</b>, etc. And as for what he said to Reuben words of rebuke, there too he commanded the blessing, after the manner of what they said (Sifrei, Beha'alotekha): the service is destined to return to the firstborns. Accordingly, since he fixed for him the title of the firstborn, he is destined to serve in the time to come. And you will find that the righteous look ahead and gaze upon the future. And for this reason Jacob exerted himself to acquire the birthright from Esau, so that he would have no portion in the service in the time to come. And although the service was given to the Levites, they are equal and both will come to perform the sacred service, for one elevates in sanctity and does not descend. And Simeon and Levi too he blessed with what he said ״אָרוּר אַפָּם״ — that is their blessing: he cursed to weaken the corrupter and the one who does harm, and likewise what he divided them and scattered them is for the attainment of the goal of happiness as well." "<b>according to his blessing.</b> Meaning, what is fitting for him according to the level of his soul and according to his deeds, for you must know that the souls — each one has a particular spiritual level: there are those whose level is priesthood, and there is kingship, and there is the crown of Torah, and there is might, and there is wealth, and there is success. And Jacob intended in prophecy to bless each one according to the blessing fitting for it: the king with kingship and the priest with priesthood, and so on in this manner, and he did not invert the paths." "<b>he blessed them, etc.</b> It says <b>them</b> in the plural, for the blessing of each and every one would benefit himself and all his brothers, as you will say: when he blesses the king that his hand will be on the neck of his enemies, behold, the good reaches all his brothers. And likewise when the greatness of one's virtue and his abundance and his elevation increase, to all his brothers as well some measure of the matter reaches. Therefore it says <b>each man according to his blessing he blessed them.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:12.965916076Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}